\begin_layout Standard
It has been claimed that “Eskimos” have no word for snow;
 instead they distinguish between dozens or even hundreds of varieties.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This is,
 of course,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "bullshit"
target "https://languagelog.ldc.upenn.edu/nll/?p=4419"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 a cliché snatched out of the chilly air to promote the idea that language itself shapes and constrains how we perceive the world.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Not helped by the fact that Inuit is polysynthetic—
so you can join up syllables to make as many words as you want.
 Many languages (including English) have many words for snow.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 A strong version is the Sapir-Whorf hypothesis,
 otherwise known as “linguistic relativism”.
 It’s just Orwell’s Newspeak,
 where shaping the language appropriately prevents dissidents from even articulating their desires and dissatisfaction.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We at the Ministry of Truth naturally disparage this sort of idea :)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Of course,
 people will find a way of expressing themselves,
 and the glorious thing about language is that it allows you to combine bits and pieces into new,
 powerful constructs.
 This doesn’t necessarily imply that all languages are equal in this regard,
 particularly when it comes to computer languages.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let’s now explore flow of control in my esoteric language 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "Zyzyxia"
target "https://drjo.substack.com/p/the-naming"
literal "false"

\end_inset

—
but through the lens of past languages.
 We’ll spelunk the deeper caverns of ancient computer science.
 I’ll try to show that gaps arise from the underlying substrate,
 and permeate the language,
 which then shapes our thinking.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
I guess we can still dream about Sapir-Whorf,
 but wearing wings is pointless if you can’t fly.
 If we’re going to embrace a cliché,
 perhaps this is a better one:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote

\emph on
A fish has no word for water.
\end_layout

