\begin_layout Section*
The substrate
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A petaflop is 
\begin_inset Formula $10^{15}$
\end_inset

 floating point operations per second.
 Today,
 we have single AI chips that can do tens of petaflops;
 this and vast troves of memory are our current ‘water.’ It was not always so.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Back before 1960,
 most computer logic was done using increasingly baroque vacuum tubes and memory was strange and constrained.
 IBM’s first commercial scientific computer was the IBM 701 in 1952.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The central processing unit was indeed made of vacuum tube logic,
 with memory provided by seventy-two 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "Williams tubes"
target "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Williams_tube"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The way they implement RAM is bizarrely awesome,
 recording each bit as a transient dot of charge on a cathode ray screen.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 each capable of holding just 1024 bits of data.
 That’s a total memory (RAM) of 2k words,
 each word storing 36 bits.
 Monthly rental in today’s cash was about $150k or a bit more,
 running for 40 hours per week.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It’s now tempting to explore the inner workings—
how each of those 36 bit words could store two 18-bit instructions,
 how numbers were formatted,
 the fact that this expensive machine had just two registers for actually doing stuff,
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The 38 bit accumulator and the 36-bit multiplier/quotient register.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 accessory storage,
 and so on—
but let’s step back.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
You can see that if a small amount of processing capacity is so precious,
 then certain human behaviours will naturally emerge.
 I’ve 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "previously noted"
target "https://drjo.substack.com/i/213376055/the-curious-incident-of-the-doc-in-the-night"
literal "false"

\end_inset

⌘ how valuable CPU time was on an IBM/360 when I first gained access decades later in 1978.
 Even then,
 on a transistor machine with 16 Megabytes of core memory,
 most jobs were batched.
 You prepared your program,
 you ran your program through,
 you examined the output for where you’d stuffed up;
 rinse and repeat.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There was no reason to pause.
 But once you’re interacting with a powerful computer,
 as everyone with a smartphone does every day now,
 there’s every reason for code to defer,
 and wait,
 and resume.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Angel wings:
 PEND
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quotation
A tuxedo and a shiny three thirty-five.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quotation
You can see it in his face,
 the blue[s] never lie.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quotation
Tonight everybody’s getting their angel wings.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quotation
And don’t you know we’re riding with the king?—
John Hiatt,
 “
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "Riding with the king"
target "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=L7Ls8ceHxhc"
literal "false"

\end_inset

”
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So Lo!
 We now can wait.
 Rudimentary attempts at this arose naturally when multi-tasking systems like 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "Multics"
target "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multics"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 were made in the late 1960s—
and from imitation and wordplay Unix was born,
 with sleep an option;
 cron 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "emerged"
target "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/linux-unix/cron-command-in-linux-with-examples/"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 in 1979.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the late 1960s,
 Simula introduced delays and even the idea of a ‘callback’—
we do something,
 and later after the task is done,
 the callback fires.
 This ties in very nicely to another idea that only seems obvious in retrospect:
 functions as first-class citizens.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Some languages are built around the idea that structure trumps everything else.
 There’s a beautiful rant from 2006 by Steve Yegge called “
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "Execution in the Kingdom of the Nouns"
target "https://steve-yegge.blogspot.com/2006/03/execution-in-kingdom-of-nouns.html"
literal "false"

\end_inset

”,
 where he explores the story of Evil King Java,
 who constrained all verbs and made their status second-class.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In contrast,
 in many Lisp-like languages,
 and Lisp itself,
 verbs are emancipated.
 They get to move around and smell the roses,
 and can be called on as needed,
 without being chaperoned in an object-oriented,
 noun-centric world.
 This makes it trivial to do things like pass a verb (function name) to another verb,
 which then schedules it to be run at some point.
 I’ve run with this in 
\series bold
\emph on
Zyzyxia
\series default
\emph default
,
 where you can say this:
\end_layout

