This approach is a trade-off between capability and danger.
 Clearly,
 if you have the ability to say something like EXECUTE <arbitrary code here>,
 then there’s more than a sporting chance that someone will use this to execute <dangerous code here>,
 and safeguards become a bit fraught.
 Early on,
 Lisp came up with eval,
 which is similar in spirit and its level of tameness,
 because eval requires a list and can’t work on an arbitrary text.
\end_layout

