\begin_layout Subsection*
We’ll start with spaghetti
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In September 2007,
 Jean Bookout and her friend Barbara Schwarz were exiting Interstate Highway 69 in Oklahoma,
 when she became unable to control the throttle.
 The car sped away despite her braking hard;
 she pulled on the parking brake.
 The 2005 Toyota Camry sped on,
 crashing into an embankment.
 Barbara died;
 Jean spent months in hospital.
 A huge underlying problem was the mess of ‘spaghetti code’ in the vehicle’s critical control system.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quotation

\emph on
Q.
 Let’s go to the next slide please.
 Tell me generally what spaghetti code means in your industry?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quotation

\emph on
A.
 Well,
 in a nutshell it means that the code is very difficult to read and maintain.
 You heard Mr.
 Ishii say that NASA had trouble reading Toyota’s source code.
 That wasn’t to do with them not following NISRA,
 it’s because it was badly written and badly structured source code.
 And that’s spaghetti.

\emph default
 —
 Trial transcript,
 14 October 2013,
 Case No CJ-2008-7869.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If we go back to the original languages that were kicking around in the early 1960s,
 they had an obvious something we pretty much never see today.
 Every language came with some sort of GOTO statement.
 It might have been GO TO (in Fortran AND Cobol);
 in ALGOL 60 it actually was GOTO (with the alternative GO TO also 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "sometimes acceptable"
target "https://retrocomputing.stackexchange.com/questions/19779/what-is-the-story-behind-goto-vs-go-to-in-algol-60"
literal "false"

\end_inset

);
 even Lisp had a GO.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
These are also all “fish/water” ideas—
they translate directly back to the underlying processor behaviour,
 where it’s natural for the executing code to branch off to another place in memory.
 The problem with GOTO is spaghetti.
 You jump somewhere—
and then you test and jump somewhere else,
 and you end up going back and forth like a bumblebee on methamphetamine.
 Anyone reading your code starts with nystagmus and ends up with a headache.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Everyone knew this,
 but in 1968 grumpy old Edsger Dijkstra burnt it into every brain with “
\emph on
Go To Statement Considered Harmful
\emph default
”.
 It’s 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "quite a rant"
target "https://homepages.cwi.nl/~storm/teaching/reader/Dijkstra68.pdf"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 but also entirely sensible.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
A fascinating aside is that originally,
 the piece was called “A case against the goto statement”.
 Niklaus Wirth came up with the final,
 fabulous title!
\end_layout

\end_inset

 In passing,
 he mentions the recent invention of the ‘CASE’ statement by two other pioneers,
 Tony Hoare and Niklaus Wirth,
 both of whom were working on ALGOL variants.
 But many people had other ideas.
\end_layout

